  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1015 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nVery light rain overnight, light again Saturday afternoon into \nSaturday night\n\nBelt of very light rain has been sliding eastward this evening ahead \nof a longwave upper trough seen on IR over MN as of 03z. Weak \nforcing overall and no instability to speak of has led to very low \nrain rates with many sites struggling to get to 0.01\". Have thus \ncontinued to go with a moderate PoP but very low QPF forecast for \nthe rain overnight tonight.\n\nShort lived upper height rises will lead to a dry Friday but, by \nSaturday, another longwave ridge moves over the upper Midwest with a \nlead impulse slowly rolling eastward around midday and a second \nwave during the nighttime hours. Progged moisture remains an issue \neast of the Mississippi due the influence of a dry high pressure \nsystem in the Great Lakes and very dry mid-levels and somewhat dry \nconditions near the surface should eat into rain totals, if any. \nFarther west, enough moisture should (70-80%) sneak around the \nsouthwestern edge of the high to lead to light rain with weak \nforcing and lack of instability once again keeping rain intensity \nand concerns for hazards for those outdoors looking ahead to October \nvery low.\n\nNext Week: Mild, Widespread Rain Likely Tuesday\n\nContinued southeasterly surface flow will allow for some ever so \nslight warming into Monday with highs potentially reaching into the \n70s for more of the area. However, dry conditions on Monday will \ngive way to increasing probabilities for widespread rainfall on \nTuesday as a longwave trough and associated surface cold front will \nsweep through the local area throughout the day on Tuesday with \nalready fairly high probabilities (50-70%) for rainfall amounts over \n0.25\" across much of the area in the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble). Much of these higher probabilities are \nin part due to a strong axis of 850mb moisture transport surging \nnorth out ahead of this front with anomalous precipitable waters as \nshown in grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) with respectable \nprobabilities (40-70%) for precipitable waters over 1.5\" on Tuesday. \nSo could certainly see some healthy rainfall totals from this \nsystem, particularly if we can get some elevated instability to work \nits way into the region. \n\nForecast confidence begins to lessen as we head into Wednesday and \nbeyond as this moisture fetch will battle against the northwesterly \ncooler and drier air in light of the aforementioned front. The key \nmechanisms for precipitation potential mid-week will be tied to a \nfairly robust upper-level trough ejecting into the south-central \nGreat Plains during the second half of the work week. This system \nwill likely see a bit of a \"boost\" in moisture as well given it will \nbe ingesting moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Polo in the \nsouthwest CONUS. As a result, noting a footprint in the Shift of \nTails of 0 in the EC Extreme Forecast Index QPF for Wednesday, \nsuggesting there are a few EC members showing QPF reaching the 99th \npercentile of climatology for this time of year. Much too early to \ndeduce if the local area may see additional rainfall from this \nfeature as it may remain too our south, but something to monitor \nnevertheless.",
